 that models treat all inputs with fair weighting.\ndf[[\'v\', \'d\', \'i\']] = (df[[\'v\', \'d\', \'i\']] - df[[\'v\', \'d\', \'i\']].min()) / (df[[\'v\', \'d\', \'i\']].max() - df[[\'v\', \'d\', \'i\']].min())\n```end\n\n```python\n# Creating binned categorical columns based on "loc"\n# Feature description: Generate bins for lines of code (loc) to classify modules into low, medium, or high code lengths.\n# Usefulness: Dividing loc into categories captures coarse-grain structural differences without overfitting to exact values.\ndf[\'loc_binned\'] = pd.cut(df[\'loc\'], bins=[0, 10, 50, df[\'loc\'].max()], labels=[\'small\', \'medium\', \'large\'])\n```end', "Here are the next three steps for feature engineering to improve defect prediction:\n\n### Step 1: Combining complexity metrics\nCreate a feature that combines key complexity metrics to capture interactions among them. Specifically, use McCabe's cyclomatic complexity (`v(g)`), essential complexity (`ev(g)`), and design complexity (`iv(g)`) to create an aggregated complexity score.\n\n```python\n# Feature name: combined_complexity_score\n# Feature description: Aggregated complexity score combining cyclomatic complexity (v(g)), essential complexity (ev(g)), and design complexity (iv(g)) using their sum scaled by code lines (loc).\ncombined_complexity_score = (df['v(g)'] + df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']) / df['loc']\ndf['combined_complexity_score'] = combined_complexity_score\n# Usefulness: This feature helps capture the overall complexity of a software module relative to its size. Modules with high complexity relative to their size might be more error-prone.\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Interaction term between Halstead volume (`v`) and defect_density\nAdd an interaction term between Halstead Volume (`v`) and `defect_density` to assess the cumulative impact of complexity and prior defect information on error likelihood.\n\n```python\n# Feature name: halstead_defect_interaction\n# Feature description: Interaction between Halstead volume (v) and defect density measures, approximating how code size and earlier defects could jointly affect future defects.\nhalstead_defect_interaction = df['v'] * df['defect_density']\ndf['halstead_defect_interaction'] = halstead_defect_interaction\n# Usefulness: Halstead volume captures part of the code complexity, while defect density reflects prior defect presence. Combining these can reveal modules prone to future defects due to their inherent quality issues.\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Rolling Average of defect density\nUse a rolling average (e.g., over previous 5 modules) for `defect_density` to capture trends in defect occurrence over neighboring modules in workflows or historical snapshots.\n\n```python\n# Feature name: rolling_defect_density\n# Feature description: Compute a rolling mean of defect density across previous modules to reflect temporal trends or local patterns in defect occurrence.\nrolling_defect_density = df['defect_density'].rolling(window=5, min_periods=1).mean()\ndf['rolling_defect_density'] = rolling_defect_density\n# Usefulness: Defect rates often cluster in certain periods or adjacent modules due to development cycles, shared code dependencies, or repeated team errors. A rolling metric helps identify these patterns.\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns\nRemove a column if it provides redundant information or fails to contribute to predictive power.\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\n# lOBlank only provides information about blank lines, which has little predictive power given the other features like loc (total lines of code) and lOCode (actual code lines). It adds noise to the model rather than useful variance.\n```end"]
