mplexity alone may not always indicate defect-proneness, but modules with both high loc and complexity might have a compounding effect on defects. Capturing this interaction allows the model to pick up critical relationships between size and complexity.\ndf['interaction_loc_v_g'] = df['loc'] * df['v(g)']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: normalized_comment_to_code_ratio\n# Feature description: Z-score normalized version of the comment_to_code_ratio feature. This transformation normalizes the feature to have a mean of 0 and standard deviation of 1.\n# Usefulness: Comment-to-code ratio has a heavily skewed and sparse distribution (with most values concentrated at 0). Normalizing it helps the model weigh the contribution of this feature fairly, particularly since most machine learning models perform better with scaled features.\ndf['normalized_comment_to_code_ratio'] = (df['comment_to_code_ratio'] - df['comment_to_code_ratio'].mean()) / df['comment_to_code_ratio'].std()\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column 'comment_to_code_ratio' is dropped\n# The original comment_to_code_ratio is highly skewed and sparse (Q1, Median, Q3 = 0). We are replacing it with a normalized version that works better for many machine learning models.\ndf.drop(columns=['comment_to_code_ratio'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
