### Step 1: Add Interaction Term Between loc and v(g)
python
# loc_vg_interaction
# Feature description: This feature captures the interaction between lines of code (loc) and cyclomatic complexity (v(g)). It is calculated as loc * v(g) and represents the total impact of both code size and logical complexity on the defect likelihood.
# Usefulness: Larger modules with higher logical complexity are more prone to defects. By synthesizing the multiplication of loc and v(g), this feature highlights combinations where both factors together contribute significantly to defect generation.
df['loc_vg_interaction'] = df['loc'] * df['v(g)']
end

---

### Step 2: Create Weighted Comment-to-Code Ratio
python
# weighted_comment_to_code_ratio
# Feature description: This feature is an enhancement of the existing comment_to_code_ratio by weighting it against the loc. It is calculated as (comment_to_code_ratio * loc) / (1 + loc). The weighting ensures that small modules with anomalously high comment density don't overly influence the defect prediction.
# Usefulness: As comments are often used to clarify complex parts of the code, this feature might help in identifying sections of code where comments are used to compensate for higher complexity or potential defects.
df['weighted_comment_to_code_ratio'] = (df['comment_to_code_ratio'] * df['loc']) / (1 + df['loc'])
end

---

### Step 3: Add Feature to Measure Design Complexity Normalized by Code Length
python
# normalized_design_complexity
# Feature description: This feature calculates design complexity (iv(g)) per line of code: iv(g) / loc. This measure normalizes the design complexity of the module by its size.
# Usefulness: Design complexity per unit of code helps identify cases where a small amount of code has disproportionately complex designs, potentially leading to defect zones.
df['normalized_design_complexity'] = df['iv(g)'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-6)
end

